#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179543 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179684 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52e02202-ebdf-5987-bbaf-b5eb204c3892" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Radical Function With an Even Index"}] :question/id #uuid "7096f867-7d37-5630-a885-d0a4f4a6acb7" :question/math-academy-id "e-8142" :question/problem "What is the range of $f(x) = -2\\sqrt[4]{1-2x}+1$?" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\sqrt[4] x$ is\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[4] x \\ge 0\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches do not affect the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[4]{1-2x} \\ge 0\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-2$ and then adding $1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt[4]{1 - 2x} & \\ge 0 \\\\ -2\\sqrt[4]{1 - 2x} & \\le 0 \\\\ -2\\sqrt[4]{1 - 2x} + 1 & \\le 1 \\\\ f(x) & \\le 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x) \\in (-\\infty,1]$.\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/3c/3ce03253fe40abe25d69866d5b52a621168e3dab2a45ca33d951f1a4551b98c9.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179546 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179687 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3abc2f98-9bbd-5a33-87fc-f9459fb18c60" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Radical Function With an Odd Index"}] :question/id #uuid "9af3bd0c-c209-508f-b806-bb9426f2c0da" :question/math-academy-id "e-8719" :question/problem "What is the range of $f(x) = -3\\sqrt[3]{5-2x}+6$?" :question/worked-solution "The range of\n\n$$\ny = \\sqrt[3] x\n$$\n\nis\n$y \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\nTransformations do not change this interval. Therefore, the range of $f(x)$ is $y \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$."}]}
